D\\) is sent by \\(\\mathcal S\\) onto the triangle \\(ADC\\) (the vertices \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are interchanged while \\(A\\) stays fixed).  \nThe circumcenter of a triangle depends only on its three vertices; therefore the circumcenter of \\(ADC\\) coincides with the circumcenter of \\(ACD\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(P)=P .\n\\]\n\nThus the whole line \\(AP\\) is pointwise fixed by \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \nA line fixed by a spiral similarity bisects the angle between any pair of corresponding lines, whence from (1)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle MAP=\\angle PAN\\;},\\qquad \n\\boxed{\\;\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD\\;}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Directed angle between \\(AP\\) and the chord \\(BE\\)\n\nAll points \\(A,B,E\\) lie on the small circle \\(\\Omega\\).  \nUsing the inscribed‑angle theorem for \\(\\Omega\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle (AP,BE) \n   &=\\angle (AP,AB)+\\angle (AB,BE)                \\\\\n   &=\\angle PAB+\\angle ABE                         \\\\\n   &=\\angle PAE .                                   \\tag{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(The last equality follows because \\(A,B,E\\) are concyclic: the angle \\(\\angle PAE\\) equals the sum of \\(\\angle PAB\\) and \\(\\angle BAE\\), and \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle ABE\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Directed angle \\(\\angle BFE\\)\n\nAll points \\(A,B,F\\) lie on the large circle \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nApplying the inscribed‑angle theorem to \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BFE \n   &=\\angle BFA+\\angle AFE                         \\\\\n   &=\\angle BFA+\\angle AFD                         \\\\\n   &=\\angle BFA .                                   \\tag{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(The equality \\(\\angle AFE=\\angle AFD\\) holds because \\(F,D,A\\) are collinear.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Equality of the two angles\n\nFrom (2) we have \\(\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD\\). Adding \\(\\angle PAE\\) to both sides gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PAE = \\angle PAE +\\angle CAP-\\angle PAD\n           = \\angle PAE +\\angle CAP-\\angle PAD .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle PAE =\\angle PAB+\\angle BAE\\) and, using \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle NAE\\) (the ray \\(AB\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\)), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle PAE \n   &=\\angle PAN+\\angle NAE                         \\\\\n   &=\\angle MAP+\\angle NAE                         \\\\\n   &=\\angle BFA .                                   \\tag{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(The last step uses that the ray \\(NA\\) is taken by \\(\\mathcal S\\) onto the ray \\(MA\\); consequently \\(\\angle NAE=\\angle BFA\\).)\n\nCombining (3), (4) and (5) we arrive at  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  From the angle equality to the required tangency\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through an arbitrary point \\(X\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBecause parallelism preserves directed angles,  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell,BE)=\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side is precisely the angle between the chord \\(BE\\) and the tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(E\\) (or at \\(F\\); both are equal).  \nHence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to that circumcircle.\n\nChoosing \\(X=H\\), the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), we obtain that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved, using only elementary properties of spiral similarity, circumcenters and inscribed angles, that  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is 