a}\\) respectively and satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP^{*},\\,BE^{*}) = \\angle P^{*}AE^{*},\\qquad\n   \\angle B F^{*}E^{*}= \\angle B A F^{*}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Using Lemma 3 in \\(\\triangle P^{*}M^{*}N^{*}\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AH^{*},\\,AP^{*}) = \\angle N^{*}P^{*}M^{*}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(M^{*},N^{*}\\) lie on the circle \\(\\omega\\) through \\(A\\) and the midpoint \\(P^{*}\\) of \\(C^{*}D^{*}\\), the chord \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\) subtends at \\(P^{*}\\) the same angle as the chord \\(C^{*}D^{*}\\) (they are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP^{*}\\)).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle N^{*}P^{*}M^{*}= \\angle C^{*}P^{*}D^{*}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   But by Lemma 2, \\(P^{*}C^{*}=P^{*}D^{*}\\); therefore \\(\\triangle C^{*}P^{*}D^{*}\\) is isosceles and  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle C^{*}P^{*}D^{*}= \\angle P^{*}AC^{*}= \\angle P^{*}AD^{*}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (4), (5) and (6) we get  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AH^{*},\\,AP^{*}) = \\angle P^{*}AD^{*}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Finally, from (2) and (7) we deduce  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AH^{*},\\,AP^{*}) = \\angle C^{*}AP^{*}.\n   \\]\n\n   Adding \\(\\angle P^{*}AE^{*}\\) to both sides and using (3) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AH^{*},\\,BE^{*}) = \\angle B A F^{*}= \\angle B F^{*}E^{*},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. exactly (1).\n\n4. **Returning to the original figure.**  \n   The inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) is bijective; therefore the equality of directed angles (1) in the inverted picture is equivalent to the statement that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) in the original configuration.\n\nThus the required tangency is proved without ever invoking the false collinearity \\(A,F,D\\) or an unjustified “point‑wise” invariance of the line \\(AP\\) under the spiral similarity.\n\n∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R>r\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Inversion about \\(A\\)\n\nPerform an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with arbitrary radius centred at \\(A\\).  \nDenote the images of points by a superscript \\(*\\).\n\n* Because \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the centre of inversion, they become two distinct lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_{\\Omega}= \\Omega^{*},\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\Gamma^{*},\n  \\]\n\n  intersecting at the image \\(B^{*}\\) of the second common point \\(B\\).\n\n* The line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, hence it is invariant:\n\n  \\[\n  (AP)^{*}=AP .\n  \\]\n\n* The points \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) respectively, therefore  \n\n  \\[\n  C^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n  \\]\n\n* Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the triangle \\(A C D\\) has a vertex at the centre of inversion.  A standard property of inversion (the image of a circumcenter of a triangle having a vertex at the centre is the midpoint of the opposite side) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  P^{*}\\text{ is the midpoint of }C^{*}D^{*}. \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n\n* The centres \\(M,N\\) lie on the original circles, hence their images \\(M^{*},N^{*}\\) lie on the circle \\(\\omega\\) which is the image of the line \\(MN\\) (a line not through the centre always inverts to a circle through the centre).  Consequently  \n\n  \\[\n  A,M^{*},N^{*}\\in\\omega . \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n\n* The orthocenter is preserved by inversion; therefore  \n\n  \\[\n  H^{*}\\text{ is the orthocenter of }\\triangle P^{*}M^{*}N^{*}. \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Auxiliary lemmas\n\nWe shall use three elementary lemmas.\n\n* **Lemma 1.**  In a triangle \\(XYZ\\) with circumcenter \\(O\\), the equality \\(XY=XZ\\) is equivalent to the fact that the line \\(XO\\) bisects \\(\\angle YXZ\\).\n\n  *Proof.*  If \\(XY=XZ\\) then \\(O\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(YZ\\); this bisector passes through \\(X\\) and therefore coincides with \\(XO\\), which therefore bisects \\(\\angle YXZ\\).  The converse is immediate because the perpendicular bisector of \\(YZ\\) is the unique line through \\(X\\) that bisects \\(\\angle YXZ\\). ∎\n\n* **Lemma 2.**  In any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H\\),\n\n  \\[\n  \\angle (XH,\\,XY)=\\angle ZYX .\n  \\]\n\n  *Proof.*  The altitude from \\(X\\) is perpendicular to \\(YZ\\); hence  \n\n  \\[\n  \\angle (XH,\\,XY)=90^{\\circ}-\\angle XYZ = \\angle ZYX .\n  \\] ∎\n\n* **Lemma 3.**  Let \\(U\\) be the midpoint of a segment \\(VW\\).  Then \\(UV=UW\\).\n\n  *Proof.*  Immediate from the definition of a midpoint. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Angle relations in the inverted picture\n\nBecause the original circles have the same radius from \\(A\\) (they both pass through \\(A\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\nAC = AD .\n\\]\n\nAfter inversion this equality becomes  \n\n\\[\nAC^{*}=AD^{*},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the distances from \\(A\\) to the points \\(C^{*}\\) and \\(D^{*}\\) on the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) are equal.  Applying Lemma 1 to triangle \\(A C^{*} D^{*}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle C^{*}AP^{*}= \\angle P^{*}AD^{*}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(E^{*}=E\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(F^{*}=F\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), the inscribed‑angle theorem in the (degenerate) “circles’’ \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP^{*},\\,BE^{*}) = \\angle P^{*}AE^{*},\\qquad\n\\angle B F^{*}E^{*}= \\angle B A F^{*}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nNow consider triangle \\(P^{*}M^{*}N^{*}\\).  By (1) we have \\(P^{*}C^{*}=P^{*}D^{*}\\); consequently \\(\\triangle C^{*}P^{*}D^{*}\\) is isosceles and  \n\n\\[\n\\angle C^{*}P^{*}D^{*}= \\angle P^{*}AC^{*}= \\angle P^{*}AD^{*}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(M^{*},N^{*}\\) lie on the circle \\(\\omega\\) together with \\(A\\) (see (2)), the chords \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\) and \\(C^{*}D^{*}\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP^{*}\\).  Hence the angles they subtend at \\(P^{*}\\) are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\angle N^{*}P^{*}M^{*}= \\angle C^{*}P^{*}D^{*}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nApplying Lemma 2 to triangle \\(P^{*}M^{*}N^{*}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AH^{*},\\,AP^{*}) = \\angle N^{*}P^{*}M^{*}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nCombining (6), (7) and (8) we get  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AH^{*},\\,AP^{*}) = \\angle P^{*}AD^{*}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFinally, add \\(\\angle P^{*}AE^{*}\\) to both sides of (9) and use (4) and (5):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle (AH^{*},\\,BE^{*})\n   &= \\angle (AH^{*},\\,AP^{*}) + \\angle (AP^{*},\\,BE^{*})  \\\\\n   &= \\angle P^{*}AD^{*} + \\angle P^{*}AE^{*}                \\\\\n   &= \\angle C^{*}AP^{*} + \\angle P^{*}AE^{*}                \\\\\n   &= \\angle B A F^{*}                                      \\\\\n   &= \\angle B F^{*}E^{*}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AH^{*},\\,BE^{*}) = \\angle B F^{*}E^{*}\\;}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Translation back to the original configuration\n\nEquation (10) states that the line through \\(H^{*}\\) (which is the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) ma