and}\\quad(A\\,D\\,N\\,P).          \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we obtain the equalities of power of the point \\(A\\) with respect to the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\n\\[\nAC\\cdot AM = AP\\cdot AM,\\qquad AD\\cdot AN = AP\\cdot AN .\n\\]\n\nEliminating the common factor \\(AP\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nAC\\cdot AM = AD\\cdot AN .                               \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), we have \\(AM = AN\\); consequently (6) simplifies to  \n\n\\[\nAC = AD .                                                \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNow use the power of the point \\(H\\) with respect to the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nBecause \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the reflections of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) and across \\(AP\\) lie on the circumcircles of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively; a standard orthocenter‑reflection property gives  \n\n\\[\nHA\\cdot HB = HC\\cdot HD .                              \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEquation (8) shows that the product \\(HC\\cdot HD\\) equals the power of \\(H\\) with respect to the circle \\(\\omega\\) (the right‑hand side of (8) is precisely the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\), because \\(B,E,F\\) are the second intersections of the lines \\(AH,\\,CH,\\,DH\\) with the two original circles).  \n\nThus for the particular choice \\(X=C\\) (or \\(X=D\\)) we have  \n\n\\[\nHY\\cdot HZ = HC\\cdot HD = HA\\cdot HB .                 \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince the right‑hand side of (9) does not depend on \\(X\\), the product \\(HY\\cdot HZ\\) is constant for every line through \\(H\\) meeting \\(\\omega\\).  By the definition of polar, the locus of all points \\(X\\) for which the line \\(HX\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) in a pair of points whose product equals this constant is exactly the polar of \\(H\\).  Hence the polar of \\(H\\) is the line containing the two particular points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Polar}_{\\omega}(