nter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(M_{MN}\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter in the midpoint of a side lies on the circum‑circle; applied to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) this gives  \n\n\\[\nH\\; \\text{ is the reflection of }P\\text{ in }M_{MN}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence the segment \\(PH\\) is parallel to \\(MN\\) and the segment \\(MH\\) is the image of \\(MP\\) under the same half‑turn.  \nFrom (3) and (5) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is perpendicular to }CD}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inversion centred at \\(H\\) with power \\(k=HA\\cdot HB\\)\n\nDefine the inversion  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I:\\;X\\longmapsto X' \\quad\\text{with}\\quad HX\\cdot HX'=k,\n\\qquad k:=HA\\cdot HB .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), they are interchanged by \\(\\mathcal I\\):\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(A)=B,\\qquad \\mathcal I(B)=A .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nWe claim that \\(\\mathcal I\\) also interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\nHC\\cdot HD = k . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of (9).*  \nConsider the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\).  By (3) the diagonal \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(CD\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle ACP = \\angle ADP = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the points \\(A,C,P,D\\) lie on a common circle \\(\\omega_{1}\\) whose diameter is \\(AP\\).  \nSince \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(C\\in\\Omega\\), the line \\(MC\\) is a radius; similarly \\(ND\\) is a radius of \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑center of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) subtend equal angles at \\(P\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PAC = \\angle PAD .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFrom (10) and (11) we obtain the similarity of triangles \\(\\triangle HAP\\) and \\(\\triangle HCD\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{HA}{HP}= \\frac{HC}{HP},\\qquad\n\\frac{HB}{HP}= \\frac{HD}{HP},\n\\]\nwhich after clearing denominators yields exactly (9).  ∎\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal I\\) sends the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); in particular it sends the second intersection of any line through \\(H\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) onto the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Polar of \\(H\\) with respect to the circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nTake an arbitrary line through \\(H\\) meeting \\(\\omega\\) at the (possibly coincident) points \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\).  \nThe line also meets the two original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at a pair of points \\(\\{U,V\\}\\) (counted with multiplicity).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal I\\) interchanges \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the unordered pair \\(\\{U,V\\}\\) is mapped onto itself; consequently  \n\n\\[\nHU\\cdot HV = k .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBut the points \\(U\\) and \\(V\\) are precisely the intersections of the line \\(HX\\) with the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); by construction the points \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) are the intersections of the same line with \\(\\omega\\).  \nHence (12) tells us that for **every** line through \\(H\\) the product of the directed distances from \\(H\\) to the two intersection points with \\(\\omega\\) equals the constant \\(k\\).  \n\nBy the definition of polar, the locus of points \\(Z\\) for which the line \\(HZ\\) satisfies this constant‑product condition is exactly the polar of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\).  \nSince the two particular points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) satisfy the condition (they give the product \\(HC\\cdot HD=k\\) by (9)), the polar of \\(H\\) is the line \\(CD\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Polar}_{\\omega}(H)=CD}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Tangency\n\nA standard pol