pported.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Verification Log\n\nBelow each quoted passage from the proposed solution is followed by a rigorous analysis.\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Claim that \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)\n\n> \u201cFrom (2) we have \\(PC=PD\\); hence \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). Because also \\(PA=PC\\), the point \\(A\\) belongs to the same bisector, so the whole line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The set of points equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is the perpendicular bisector of segment \\(CD\\).  \n- \\(PC=PD\\) indeed places \\(P\\) on that bisector.  \n- To conclude that **\\(A\\) also lies on the bisector**, we would need \\(AC=AD\\). The equality \\(PA=PC\\) (or \\(PA=PD\\)) does **not** imply \\(AC=AD\\). Hence the inference that \\(A\\) belongs to the perpendicular bisector is false.  \n- Consequently the statement \u201c\\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)\u201d is incorrect.\n\n**Result:** **Critical Error** \u2013 This false geometric relation is used in every subsequent step (perpendicularity, polar identification, etc.). The logical chain collapses here.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Consequences drawn from the false perpendicular bisector claim\n\n> \u201cConsequently  \n> \\[\n> AP\\perp CD,\\qquad M_{CD}\\in AP,\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nSince the premise that \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector is false, the derived orthogonality \\(AP\\perp CD\\) and the claim that the midpoint of \\(CD\\) lies on \\(AP\\) are both unfounded.\n\n**Result:** **Critical Error** \u2013 These statements rely entirely on the previous invalid claim.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Reflection of the orthocenter in the midpoint of a side\n\n> \u201cIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter in the midpoint of a side lies on the circum\u2011circle; applied to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) this gives  \n> \\[\n> H\\; \\text{ is the reflection of }P\\text{ in }M_{MN}. \\tag{5}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The well\u2011known fact: *the reflection of the orthocenter \\(H\\) across the midpoint of a side lies on the circumcircle* is correct.  \n- However, the reflected point is **not** the opposite vertex \\(P\\); it is the point \\(P'\\) on the circumcircle such that \\(PP'\\) is a diameter.  \n- Therefore the statement that \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) is false; the correct relationship is that the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(M_{MN}\\) is the point on the circumcircle opposite \\(P\\).  \n\n**Result:** **Critical Error** \u2013 This misidentification is later used to assert that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(CD\\) (step (6)), which is consequently invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Derivation of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) being perpendicular to \\(CD\\)\n\n> \u201cFrom (3) and (5) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is perpendicular to }CD}. \\tag{6}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- (3) is the false claim that \\(AP\\perp CD\\).  \n- (5) is the false claim about the reflection of \\(P\\).  \n- Even if (5) were correct, the perpendicularity of \\(AP\\) to \\(CD\\) is not established, so the conclusion (6) has no valid foundation.\n\n**Result:** **Critical Error** \u2013 This statement is a direct consequence of earlier false premises.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Definition of the inversion centred at \\(H\\)\n\n> \u201cDefine the inversion  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal I:\\;X\\longmapsto X' \\quad\\text{with}\\quad HX\\cdot HX'=k,\n> \\qquad k:=HA\\cdot HB .\n> \\tag{7}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe definition itself is legitimate; any point \\(H\\) and any positive constant \\(k\\) define an inversion. No error here.\n\n**Result:** **Correct** (provided later use is justified).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Claim that the inversion swaps \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)\n\n> \u201cBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), they are interchanged by \\(\\mathcal I\\):  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal I(A)=B,\\qquad \\mathcal I(B)=A .\n> \\tag> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- For an inversion centred at \\(H\\) with power \\(k=HA\\cdot HB\\), the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are indeed inverse of each other **iff** they are collinear with \\(H\\) and satisfy \\(HA\\cdot HB = k\\).  \n- The solution never proves that \\(H, A, B\\) are collinear, nor that \\(HA = HB\\). The only given relation is the definition of \\(k\\). Without collinearity, an inversion does **not** map a point to another arbitrary point on the same circle.  \n- Hence the assertion that the inversion swaps \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) is unsupported.\n\n**Result:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 The step assumes a property of the inversion that has not been demonstrated.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Claim that the inversion also swaps \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)\n\n> \u201cWe claim that \\(\\mathcal I\\) also interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); i.e.  \n> \\[\n> HC\\cdot HD = k . \\tag{9}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The proof offered relies on (10) and (11) to claim similarity of \\(\\triangle HAP\\) and \\(\\triangle HCD\\).  \n- (10) states \\(\\angle ACP = \\angle ADP = 90^{\\circ}\\) \u2013 this follows from the (incorrect) assertion that \\(AP\\perp CD\\). Since (10) is already based on a false premise, the subsequent reasoning is invalid.  \n- Even ignoring that, the angles given do not establish the required similarity: we would need two pairs of equal angles between the triangles, but the presented equalities involve different vertices and do not match.  \n- Consequently the equality \\(HC\\cdot HD = HA\\cdot HB\\) is not proved.\n\n**Result:** **Justification Gap** (and also dependent on earlier Critical Errors).\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Consequence that the inversion sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\n> \u201cThus \\(\\mathcal I\\) sends the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); in particular it sends the second intersection of any line through \\(H\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) onto the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- This conclusion presumes that the inversion maps every point of \\(\\Omega\\) to a point of \\(\\Gamma\\). For that to hold, the two circles must be *inverse* with respect to \\(\\mathcal I\\), i.e., the centre \\(H\\) must lie on the radical axis of the two circles and the product of distances from \\(H\\) to the two circles must be constant. The solution never establishes these conditions; it merely attempts (and fails) to show that two specific pairs of points (\\(A,B\\) and \\(C,D\\)) are interchanged.  \n- Therefore the claim is unfounded.\n\n**Result:** **Critical Error** \u2013 The mapping of the whole circles is not justified.\n\n---\n\n### 9.  Identification of the polar of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) as line \\(CD\\)\n\n> \u201cSince the two particular points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) satisfy the condition (they give the product \\(HC\\cdot HD=k\\) by (9)), the polar of \\(H\\) is the line \\(CD\\):  \n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\text{Polar}_{\\omega}(H)=CD}. \\tag{13}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The polar of a point with respect to a circle is defined as the locus of points \\(X\\) such that for the line \\(HX\\) intersecting the circle at \\(X_1,X_2\\) we have \\(HX_1\\cdot HX_2 = \\text{constant}\\) (the power of \\(H\\)).  \n- Demonstrating that **two** points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) satisfy the constant\u2011product condition does **not** prove that the entire line \\(CD\\) is the polar; the polar is a unique line, but to identify it one must show that **every** point of that line satisfies the condition, or use a known theorem (e.g., the polar is the line joining the two inverse points of any chord through \\(H\\)). The solution provides only the single pair \\(C,D\\) and no argument that the whole line consists of such points.  \n- Moreover, the equality \\(HC\\cdot HD = k\\) itself was not rigorously established (see previous gap).  \n\n**Result:** **Critical Error** \u2013 The identification of the polar is unjustified.\n\n---\n\n### 10.  Final tangency argument\n\n> \u201cA standard pole\u2013polar theorem states: If a line \\(\\ell\\) is the polar of a point \\(H\\) with respect to a circle \\(\\omega\\), then the line through \\(H\\) perpendicular to \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).  \n> Applying this to (13) we obtain that the line through \\(H\\) **perpendicular** to \\(CD\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).  \n> From (6) we already know that the line through \\(H\\) **parallel** to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(CD\\).  \n> Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The pole\u2013polar theorem is correctly stated.  \n- However, its application depends on the prior (incorrect) identification of the polar as \\(CD\\).  \n- Additionally, the claim that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(CD\\) rests on the earlier false statements (3) and (5).  \n\n**Result:** **Critical Error** \u2013 The final conclusion rests on a chain of previously invalid steps.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution collapses at the very first geometric claim (that \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)). This error propagates through the proof, rendering subsequent arguments about the orthocenter, the inversion, the polar, and the tangency invalid. Moreover, several later steps contain additional unjustified assertions (reflection of orthocenter, behavior of the inversion, identification of the polar) that would need independent rigorous proof even if the initial error were corrected.\n\n**Therefore, the solution is not correct.**"

