onsequently \\(AP\\perp CD,\\;M_{CD}\\in AP\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  \n- From \\(PC=PD\\) we correctly infer that \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \n- However, \\(PA=PC\\) only tells us that \\(A\\) is equidistant from \\(P\\) and \\(C\\); it does **not** imply \\(A\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  \n- The perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the set \\(\\{X\\mid XC=XD\\}\\). There is no reason for \\(A\\) to satisfy \\(AC=AD\\).  \n- Hence the conclusion \u201c\\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)\u201d is unfounded, and all subsequent statements that rely on \\(AP\\perp CD\\) are baseless.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Description of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\n> \u201cIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter in the midpoint of a side lies on the circum\u2011circle; applied to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) this gives \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in \\(M_{MN}\\). Hence the segment \\(PH\\) is parallel to \\(MN\\) \u2026\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  \n- In a triangle \\(XYZ\\), the reflection of the orthocenter across the midpoint of side \\(YZ\\) lies on the circumcircle, but it is **not** the vertex \\(X\\).  \n- For \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the reflection of \\(H\\) across the midpoint of \\(MN\\) is the antipode of \\(P\\) on the circumcircle, not \\(P\\) itself.  \n- Therefore the statement \u201c\\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in \\(M_{MN}\\)\u201d is false. Consequently the derived parallelism \\(PH\\parallel MN\\) and the later claim that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(CD\\) (which already relied on the false perpendicular\u2011bisector claim) are unsupported.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Construction of an inversion centred at \\(H\\)\n\n> \u201cDefine the inversion \\(\\mathcal I:\\;X\\mapsto X'\\) with \\(HX\\cdot HX'=k,\\;k:=HA\\cdot HB.\\) Because \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), they are interchanged by \\(\\mathcal I\\): \\(\\mathcal I(A)=B,\\ \\mathcal I(B)=A.\\)\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  \n- An inversion with centre \\(H\\) and power \\(k\\) maps a point \\(X\\) to a point \\(X'\\) lying on the ray \\(HX\\) such that \\(HX\\cdot HX'=k\\).  \n- There is no guarantee that \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on the same ray from \\(H\\); in general they are not collinear with \\(H\\).  \n- Hence the assertion that the inversion swaps \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) is unjustified and false.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Claim that the inversion also swaps \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)\n\n> \u201cWe claim that \\(\\mathcal I\\) also interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); i.e. \\(HC\\cdot HD = k.\\)\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  \n- This claim is presented without proof; the subsequent \u201cproof\u201d uses the false perpendicular\u2011bisector property from step\u202f2 and other incorrect angle relations.  \n- Since the premise that \\(AP\\perp CD\\) is false, the derived equality \\(HC\\cdot HD = HA\\cdot HB\\) has no basis.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Right\u2011angle configuration in quadrilateral \\(APCD\\)\n\n> \u201cConsider the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\).  By (3) the diagonal \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(CD\\); therefore \\(\\angle ACP = \\angle ADP = 90^{\\circ}\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  \n- Statement (3) (that \\(AP\\perp CD\\)) is false, so the conclusion that \\(\\angle ACP\\) and \\(\\angle ADP\\) are right angles is invalid.\n\n---\n\n#### 7.  Claim that \\(A,C,P,D\\) are concyclic with diameter \\(AP\\)\n\n> \u201cConsequently the points \\(A,C,P,D\\) lie on a common circle \\(\\omega_{1}\\) whose diameter is \\(AP\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  \n- The right\u2011angle condition needed for a diameter theorem is absent; therefore the claim of a circle with diameter \\(AP\\) containing \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is unfounded.\n\n---\n\n#### 8.  Equality of angles \\(\\angle PAC\\) and \\(\\angle PAD\\)\n\n> \u201cBecause \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011center of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the chords \\(AC\\) and