e PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required tangency holds.}}\n\\]\n\n### Verification Feedback ###\n\n**Critical Errors (must fix):**\n\n1. **Location**: \"“From (2) we have \\(PC=PD\\); hence \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). Because also \\(PA=PC\\), the point \\(A\\) belongs to the same bisector, so the whole line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).”\"\n   **Issue**: Critical Error – From \\(PA=PC=PD\\) one cannot conclude that \\(A\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); consequently \\(AP\\) need not be the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).\n\n2. **Location**: \"“In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter in the midpoint of a side lies on the circum‑circle; applied to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) this gives \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in \\(M_{MN}\\).”\"\n   **Issue**: Critical Error – In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the reflection of the orthocenter across the midpoint of \\(MN\\) is the antipode of \\(P\\) on the circumcircle, not \\(P\\) itself. Hence \\(H\\) is not the reflection of \\(P\\).\n\n3. **Location**: \"“Define the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with power \\(k=HA\\cdot HB\\). Because \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), they are interchanged by \\(\\mathcal I\\): \\(\\mathcal I(A)=B,\\ \\mathcal I(B)=A\\).”\"\n   **Issue**: Critical Error – An inversion centred at \\(H\\) with power \\(HA\\cdot HB\\) maps a point \\(X\\) to a point \\(X'\\) on the ray \\(HX\\) satisfying \\(HX\\cdot HX'=k\\). There is no reason for \\(A\\) to be sent to \\(B\\) (they are not collinear with \\(H\\) in general).\n\n4. **Location**: \"“We claim that \\(\\mathcal I\\) also interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); i.e. \\(HC\\cdot HD = k\\).”\"\n   **Issue**: Critical Error – This claim is unsupported; the proof that follows relies on the false statements above (especially the claim that \\(AP\\perp CD\\)).